2025, day 255

Good morning from Asia.

‘US to urge G7 to impose high tariffs on China and India over Russian oil purchases’ (FT) - but didn’t the EU just refuse? The EU wants to sign an FTA with India instead and to offer to bring forward its 2027 deadline for ceasing to buy Russian energy; as

‘Trump’s India nominee pledges to pull New Delhi away from Beijing’ (SCMP) but “US president’s envoy pick, however, says India must stop buying Russian oil and wants it to open up its market for US products”;

Treasury Secretary Bessent will meet China trade negotiator VP He in Madrid to discuss trade, economic, & national security issues, with talks also covering TikTok and efforts to combat money laundering;

Former Brazil President of Bolsonaro was sentenced to 27 years over plotting a coup, to which Secretary of State Rubio stated: “The U.S. will respond accordingly to this witch hunt.”;

Lebanon stated it expects to disarm Hezbollah in 3 months - a tall order;

Israel’s PM posted in English that Qatar is harbouring terrorists and it would act against them wherever they are - and that an outraged international community did the same before with those they saw as terrorists. Israeli press reports have Qatar is so incensed it’s lobbying for the UAE to break with Israel and Egypt to militarily move against it; as

‘BBC censures staff for calling Hamas a ‘terror group’’ (Telegraph) - which is what the U.K. government designates it as; and

“Netanyahu’s office says ‘apparently the Spanish Inquisition is not enough for [Spanish PM] Sanchez,’ after counterpart laments Madrid ‘doesn’t have nuclear bombs’ to help put an end to Gaza war” (ToI);

The Philippines & Japan formally activated their joint defence pact as it also slammed China for declaring a new nature reserve in a maritime area internationally recognised as the Philippines’ territory;

U.K. ambassador to the U.S. Mandelson was sacked over emails he had sent to Epstein - ‘Why the Mandelson affair raises questions about Starmer’s judgment’ (FT) as “‘Withdrawal’ of Labour peer from America is more evidence of prime minister’s failure to pick right people, say critics”;

‘Reform vow to set up ministry for migration staffed only by civil servants who believe in secure borders’ (Daily Mail);

‘Charlie Kirk Killing Sparks Tensions and Security Fears on Capitol Hill’ (WSJ); and

‘Trump says ‘we have to beat the hell’ out of ‘radical left lunatics’ after Kirk killing’ (Politico); while

‘Oxford Union president who debated Charlie Kirk appears to celebrate shooting’ (Telegraph);

‘Hyundai should have called me for visas, Lutnick says’ (Axios);

‘Donald Trump asks appeals court to let him fire Lisa Cook before next week’s Fed meeting’ (FT) even though markets seem certain it’s time for “Rate cuts!” anyway; and

‘Trump’s going to take over the Fed’ (Politico);

‘AI can’t write good analyst research yet, says analyst’ (who wants to keep their job - have you read analyst reports?); as

‘What if the $3trn AI investment boom goes wrong?’ (Economist). Elon Musk already just stated it means the government goes bankrupt; but more generally if AI works, even white collar analysts lose their jobs —so chaos— and if it doesn’t, $3trn goes 🔥 and debt problems are unresolvable - so chaos.
